_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_ | _⚡ Quick win_

<details>
<summary>🧩 Analysis chain</summary>

🌐 Web query:

`For Google Gemini REST `models/*:generateContent`, is the model identifier expected in the URL path rather than the JSON body?`

💡 Result:

Yes, for the Google Gemini REST API `models/*:generateContent` endpoint, the model identifier is expected in the URL path rather than the JSON request body.[1][2]

Citations:

- 1: https://ai.google.dev/api/generate-content
- 2: https://docs.cloud.google.com/vertex-ai/generative-ai/docs/reference/rest/v1/projects.locations.publishers.models/generateContent

---

🏁 Script executed:

```shell
# First, locate the file and check its content
find . -name "GeminiCacheStrategy.php" -type f
```

Repository: Ultimate-Multisite/superdav-ai-agent

Length of output: 132

---

🏁 Script executed:

```shell
# Read the file to verify the claims
cat -n includes/Core/PromptCache/GeminiCacheStrategy.php
```

Repository: Ultimate-Multisite/superdav-ai-agent

Length of output: 13271

---



</details>

**Store the request URL and extract model from the Gemini path as a fallback.**

`set_request_context()` receives `$url` but only stores the API key, discarding the URL. Meanwhile, `extract_model()` reads only `$body['model']`, which is commonly omitted in Gemini `models/*:generateContent` requests—the model lives in the URL path. When the body lacks a model field, `extract_model()` returns an empty string, causing `find_or_create()` to receive an empty model identifier. This can lead to cache creation failure or incorrect resource lookups.

Additionally, the class constants `GEMINI_HOST` (line 61) and `MIN_CHARS_FOR_CACHE` (line 69) lack the required `SD_AI_AGENT_` prefix per coding guidelines.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix sketch</summary>

```diff
 private const GEMINI_HOST = 'generativelanguage.googleapis.com';

+private const SD_AI_AGENT_GEMINI_HOST = 'generativelanguage.googleapis.com';
-private const MIN_CHARS_FOR_CACHE = 128_000;
+private const SD_AI_AGENT_MIN_CHARS_FOR_CACHE = 128_000;

 private string $api_key = '';
+private string $request_url = '';

 public function set_request_context( array $headers, string $url ): void {
 	$this->api_key = $this->resolve_api_key( $headers, $url );
+	$this->request_url = $url;
 }

 private function extract_model( array $body ): string {
 	$model = $body['model'] ?? '';
-	return is_string( $model ) ? $model : '';
+	if ( is_string( $model ) && '' !== $model ) {
+		return $model;
+	}
+	$parsed = wp_parse_url( $this->request_url );
+	$path   = is_array( $parsed ) ? (string) ( $parsed['path'] ?? '' ) : '';
+	if ( preg_match( '#/models/([^:]+):generateContent#', $path, $m ) ) {
+		return $m[1];
+	}
+	return '';
 }
```

Also update references to `self::MIN_CHARS_FOR_CACHE` → `self::SD_AI_AGENT_MIN_CHARS_FOR_CACHE` (line 235).
</details>

<details>
<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>

```
Verify each finding against current code. Fix only still-valid issues, skip the
rest with a brief reason, keep changes minimal, and validate.

In `@includes/Core/PromptCache/GeminiCacheStrategy.php` around lines 184 - 186,
set_request_context currently only stores the API key and discards the incoming
$url causing extract_model to miss Gemini model identifiers embedded in the
path; update set_request_context to save the request URL (e.g.
$this->request_url) and change extract_model to, when $body['model'] is empty,
parse the saved request URL path (matching models/*:generateContent) to derive
the model name and return it so find_or_create gets a valid model identifier;
also rename the class constants GEMINI_HOST and MIN_CHARS_FOR_CACHE to include
the required SD_AI_AGENT_ prefix (e.g. SD_AI_AGENT_GEMINI_HOST and
SD_AI_AGENT_MIN_CHARS_FOR_CACHE) and update all references such as
self::MIN_CHARS_FOR_CACHE → self::SD_AI_AGENT_MIN_CHARS_FOR_CACHE accordingly.
```

</details>

